Learning GitHub Actions – Day 5: Actions
Continuing my GitHub Actions learning journey as part of my Cloud & DevOps roadmap!

📌 What are Actions in GitHub Actions?
Actions are reusable units of code that perform specific tasks within a workflow. Instead of writing every command from scratch, we can use existing actions created by GitHub or the community.
They help simplify automation and make CI/CD workflows more efficient.

🛠️ Practical Example:

name: Actions Demo

  on:
     push:
          branches:
              - main 
      
 jobs:   
     demo:     runs-on: ubuntu-latest
      steps:       
         - name: Checkout Repository         
           uses: actions/checkout@v4

        - name: Set Up Python
          uses: actions/setup-python@v5
          with: 
             python-version: '3.12' 
  
        - name: Display Python Version
           run: python --version

🔍 How it works:
uses: Calls a reusable action.
actions/checkout: Checks out repository code.
actions/setup-python: Sets up the required Python version.
with: Passes input parameters to an action.
run: Executes shell commands.

💡 Key Takeaway:
Actions help us reuse existing automation logic instead of writing everything manually, making workflows cleaner, faster, and easier to maintain.
